WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
Speaker 1: There's some super interesting
new polling that I want to talk about, about

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.380
the 2024 election and specifically about what
Republicans are thinking, and the bottom line

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:14.710
is Donald Trump's support is rapidly collapsing
among Republicans who ultimately will decide

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:20.070
is Donald Trump the 2024 Republican nominee
or is it going to be someone else?

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:26.670
Now we have seen already in January to pretty
sizable rallies.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.660
And every indication that Donald Trump is
going to continue doing more of them in March,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:36.590
he'll be speaking at the Republican National
Committee meeting some kind of mini convention

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:39.030
or event in New Orleans, Louisiana.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:45.150
This is a guy who wants to be involved in
2022, and he wants to use 2022 as a way to

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.590
ultimately have the option to be the nominee
in 2024 if he so chooses.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:55.200
Up until now, we've basically been saying
the 2024 nomination is Trump's for the taking.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.350
If he wants it, it's now a question mark,
and we're going to look at some recent data

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.640
which shows at least some Republicans seem
to be getting sick of the guy.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:09.110
And this is a very, very different situation
when one where it's Trump's if he wants it.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:14.930
The Associated Press reporting Trump facing
legal and political headwinds as he eyes a

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.320
comeback.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.900
And it references that video we showed you
last week where Trump referred to himself

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:27.920
on the golf course as the 45th and 47th president,
sort of alluding to a 2024 run.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.170
And the article points out his popularity
among Republicans is declining somewhat, with

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:39.550
71 percent saying they have a favorable opinion
of Trump compared to 78 in September.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:45.120
But the new poll shows only a narrow majority
of Republicans 56 percent want him to run

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.170
for president in 2024.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:51.570
The poll found 44 percent do not want him
to run.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:57.620
This is a very different situation to what
we had throughout most of 2021, particularly

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:05.100
the first part of the year 56 to 44 among
Republicans want him to run.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.590
That's basically 50-50.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:13.180
Now, of course, that's still fine for winning
the primary in the sense that if 56 percent

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:19.170
of Republicans support Trump and the other
44 percent support a bunch of other people,

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.160
some Ron DeSantis, some whoever, right?

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.660
Mitt Romney, Jeb Bush, Trump still wins with
that, but these are dramatically lower numbers

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.750
than we previously had.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.810
Another interesting article in The Washington
Post.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:37.849
Do Republicans love Donald Trump, as they
once did, which rates his support within the

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:43.790
party may not be quite as robust as it once
was.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.000
And that is based on a lot of the same polling.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.620
This one, though, references a Washington
Post University of Maryland survey completed

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.319
at the end of last year found that more than
six in 10 Republicans say there is evidence

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:00.440
of widespread voter fraud.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:04.550
Nearly six in 10 say Biden's election was
not legitimate.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:09.130
More Republicans say they want Trump to run
again in 2024.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.060
Then they say, want another candidate?

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:19.000
But the margins, as we talked about 50, down
to 54 to 46.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:26.390
And then lastly, there is a Marquette University
poll just looking at favorable versus unfavorable

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:27.930
on Trump.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:37.480
And if you look now, you see that Trump's
favorability is down to only 32 percent.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.270
Sixty seven percent now have an unfavorable
opinion of Donald Trump.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:46.830
Only 32 percent have a favorable opinion of
Trump.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:52.700
So there is going to be a real question here
as to whether this guy is actually viable.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:54.020
This is great news now.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.830
I don't want to praise Republicans for this.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.920
I saw some commentaries online saying Republicans
are finally coming to their senses.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.480
No, they're not.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.440
Republicans are much the same as Donald Trump,
or it's what have I heard about recently.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.870
And because Donald Trump has been sort of
out of the public spotlight for some period

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:21.049
of time, I think this is mostly an artifact
of that if there's no real alternative appealing

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:22.960
alternative to Trump.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.249
I think it's completely reasonable and even
likely that Donald Trump will be back before

00:04:28.249 --> 00:04:30.050
you know it and and popular.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:36.749
But there's a very interesting kind of intermediate
period here where if Trump's role in the 2002

00:04:36.749 --> 00:04:43.719
midterms is seeing more as a nuisance rather
than an advantage, and if some Trumpist candidates

00:04:43.719 --> 00:04:50.110
lose in twenty two, which is just nine months
from now at this point and some new Republicans

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:55.430
not at all connected to Trump win, it may
just move the Republican Party into saying,

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.430
I don't know.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.449
I think we're just kind of beyond Trump.

00:04:58.449 --> 00:04:59.449
There's nothing.

00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:03.529
Oral here were the Republicans have said we
cannot allow this again.

00:05:03.529 --> 00:05:04.529
That was really bad.

00:05:04.529 --> 00:05:06.560
It was bad for the country, it was bad for
the world.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:07.560
It was embarrassing.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.810
This is not about that.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:12.860
This is just a sort of what have you done
for me lately, top of mind type thing.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.229
And I think some Republicans are just kind
of like, Oh yeah, Trump, that guy.

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:18.789
I don't really know that I want him to run
again.

00:05:18.789 --> 00:05:23.150
And as we saw, even from the people we talked
to at the Trump rally in Conroe, Texas, over

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:28.839
the weekend, it's not that these are reasonable
Republicans, it's that they just seem to be

00:05:28.839 --> 00:05:30.830
thinking, maybe it's time for somebody else.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.930
So we're going to keep watching these numbers
really closely.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.229
But this is a significant shift away from
Trump, at least in recent polling

